# Merabetta Phase 3: Variable Product backend API
# Examples only. No requests in this file have been executed against a database.
# Replace these placeholder origins and IDs in your own authorized environment.
# Use the existing admin login/session cookie and an allowed CMS Origin.
# Authentication, CSRF/origin checks and error envelopes are inherited unchanged.
@baseUrl = https://api.example.invalid
@cmsOrigin = https://cms.example.invalid
@productId = 42
@variantId = 1000000000

# CONTRACT
# Parent fields use the existing product API's snake_case names and validation.
# Required on POST: product_code, product_name, slug, and 1-500 variants.
# product_type is forced to variable; a different type is rejected.
# details, description, short_description and product_group_code reuse the
# existing product_details persistence and full-product response helpers.
# Parent initial_inventory and stock_quantity are rejected on this API.
# Store per-variant stock_quantity inside variants instead.
# A request body may be at most 1 MB.
#
# Each new variant requires sku, attributes and regular_price.
# Optional: sale_price (null means no sale), variant_name, barcode, weight_grams,
# stock_quantity (default 0), use_product_images (default true), is_active (true).
# Prices are JSON numbers, nonnegative, at most 9999999999.99, at most 2 decimals.
# sale_price cannot exceed regular_price, including partial PATCH updates.
# Quantities/weight are integers from 0 to 2147483647; weight may be null.
# SKU: nonempty, trimmed, maximum 100 characters, case-sensitive uniqueness
# across public.product_variants, including inactive and deleted rows.
# Parent product SKUs retain the existing products-table uniqueness rules.
#
# attributes is an object of 1-20 exact master names and string options.
# Names/options must already exist in GET /api/admin/variant-attributes.
# Size, Color, Weight, Volume and Count are examples; no attribute is hardcoded.
# Names/options are case-sensitive and must not have surrounding whitespace.
# Use the existing master APIs to add an option such as XXL before using it.
# The CMS generates combinations and sends one row per combination in variants;
# this API does not invent SKUs or expand a Cartesian matrix implicitly.
# Attribute key order does not distinguish combinations.
#
# PATCH: send an id to update a variant; omit id to add one.
# Variant id and product_id are returned as strings to preserve BIGINT precision.
# Omitted variants/fields remain unchanged. An attributes object replaces that
# variant's complete selection; it is not merged one attribute at a time.
# An id-only entry is allowed. Empty variants arrays are rejected; omit variants
# for a parent-only edit. At least one field is required on PATCH.
# An unknown/foreign variant ID is rejected. IDs are never silently reassigned.
# Neither endpoint deletes variants. Deleted variants are returned by GET,
# reserve their SKU/combination, and cannot be edited or restored through PATCH.
# A SKU already held by a different variant cannot be swapped in one request.
# Unchanged stored attribute values survive unrelated edits if a master changes.
#
# GET: returns the full existing product response plus data.variants, sorted by
# variant ID, including inactive/soft-deleted rows and their original attributes.
# Parent details/images are included. Variant images are represented by the
# use_product_images flag; image uploads/galleries are outside this phase.
# Parent pricing and warehouse inventory fields retain their existing meaning;
# they are not a sum/minimum derived from variant pricing or stock.
# Decimal prices in responses are typically strings from PostgreSQL/Sequelize.
#
# TRANSACTIONS AND COMPATIBILITY
# POST/PATCH persist parent, product details, variants and the existing audit
# entry in one Sequelize transaction. Any failure rolls back all those changes.
# Writes take SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE on public.product_variants before checks to
# serialize writers without assuming Phase 2 has unique SKU/JSONB indexes.
# This can reduce concurrent variant-write throughput; normal reads can proceed.
# GET uses a repeatable-read transaction for a consistent parent/variant snapshot.
# New variant ORM operations explicitly bind schema public. The existing model
# columns match the supplied Phase 2 table; the identity seed is database-owned.
# No sync, migrations, table alterations or historical record copies are needed.
# Legacy model binding and Simple Product handlers retain their prior behavior.
# No customer catalog publishing or frontend integration is performed here.
# Live schema, indexes, grants and connectivity have not been verified.
#
# ERRORS
# 401: existing admin authentication failure.
# 403: existing origin/CSRF rejection.
# 404: missing or deleted parent product.
# 409: duplicate SKU/combination/unique parent fields; non-variable parent;
#      deleted variant edit; concurrent transaction conflict (retry).
# 422: invalid body/ID, unknown master option/reference, foreign variant ID,
#      repeated variant ID, invalid prices or database value constraints.
# 413: request exceeds the JSON body limit.
# All errors use the existing { "error": "..." } envelope; schema validation
# also includes details with field paths. Internal SQL is not returned.

### POST: create parent and CMS-generated variants (201 Created)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/variable-products
Origin: {{cmsOrigin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_code": "TEE-COTTON",
  "product_name": "Cotton tee",
  "slug": "cotton-tee",
  "product_type": "variable",
  "product_status": "draft",
  "details": {
    "full_description": "Soft cotton tee",
    "specifications": { "Material": "Cotton" }
  },
  "variants": [
    {
      "sku": "TEE-M-RED",
      "attributes": { "Size": "M", "Color": "Red" },
      "regular_price": 299,
      "sale_price": 249,
      "stock_quantity": 10,
      "use_product_images": true
    },
    {
      "sku": "TEE-L-RED",
      "attributes": { "Size": "L", "Color": "Red" },
      "regular_price": 299,
      "sale_price": null,
      "stock_quantity": 8,
      "use_product_images": true
    }
  ]
}

### GET: load editor; response includes all associated variants (200 OK)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/variable-products/{{productId}}

### PATCH: edit existing variant and add XXL, preserving omitted L (200 OK)
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/variable-products/{{productId}}
Origin: {{cmsOrigin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_name": "Cotton tee - expanded sizes",
  "variants": [
    {
      "id": "{{variantId}}",
      "sku": "TEE-M-RED-V2",
      "sale_price": 229
    },
    {
      "sku": "TEE-XXL-RED",
      "attributes": { "Size": "XXL", "Color": "Red" },
      "regular_price": 349,
      "sale_price": 299,
      "stock_quantity": 6,
      "use_product_images": true
    }
  ]
}

# Response excerpts: variable-products-responses.json in this directory.
# Offline tests (Node >=24 <27, dependencies already installed):
#   node --test scripts/variable-products.test.js
# The test suite injects in-memory persistence, and intercepts Sequelize SQL
# generation. It does not import the application/database configuration or read
# .env files. Its HTTP test only uses a temporary loopback Express server.
# Do not use npm test for this verification: that invokes unrelated suites which
# may load configuration or run database tests.
